# Drupal configuration management with Drush

Drupal's configuration sync directory is `config/sync` at the project root.
The local `web/sites/default/settings.php` points Drupal to this directory.
The settings file is intentionally ignored by Git because it can contain
machine-specific database credentials.

## First-time local setup

After creating `web/sites/default/settings.php` from
`web/sites/default/default.settings.php` (if needed), add this setting to the
local settings file. Do not replace an existing settings file, since it may
already contain this environment's database credentials:

```php
$settings['config_sync_directory'] = '../config/sync';
```

The path is relative to Drupal's `web/` directory. Each developer must set it
in their own ignored `settings.php`.

## Export the current site's configuration

Run these commands from the project root in PowerShell. Use the PHP 8.3
executable directly: `drush.bat` tries to launch through `sh`, which is not
normally installed on Windows, and a plain `php` command may resolve to an
older XAMPP installation.

```powershell
& 'C:\xampp83\php\php.exe' vendor\bin\drush.php status
& 'C:\xampp83\php\php.exe' vendor\bin\drush.php config:status
& 'C:\xampp83\php\php.exe' vendor\bin\drush.php config:export
```

If XAMPP is installed in another folder, replace `C:\xampp83\php\php.exe`
with that installation's PHP 8.3 executable.

`config:export` (alias `cex`) copies the active site's configuration from the
database to YAML files in `config/sync`. It does not import configuration or
delete or change Drupal content. Review the proposed export when Drush prompts
for confirmation, then inspect the resulting files with `git status` and
`git diff` before committing them. Do not use `--yes` unless you have reviewed
the export and intend to proceed.

The first export can create many YAML files because it records the site's
current active configuration. Review those files before adding them to Git.
The tracked `.gitkeep` only keeps the otherwise-empty sync directory present;
it is harmless and can be removed after the first export.

## On another environment

After deploying the code and importing the database backup for that same site,
review the differences first:

```powershell
& 'C:\xampp83\php\php.exe' vendor\bin\drush.php config:status
```

Only when you intentionally want the YAML in `config/sync` to replace the
active site's configuration, run:

```powershell
& 'C:\xampp83\php\php.exe' vendor\bin\drush.php config:import
```

`config:import` (alias `cim`) changes active configuration in the database.
Do not run it as part of the export workflow or without reviewing the changes.

## Git safety

The root `.gitignore` keeps `vendor/`, Drupal core and contributed code,
generated site files, local `settings.php`/`services.yml`, environment secrets,
and common SQL/database dumps out of Git. Custom project code and reviewed
configuration YAML under `config/sync` remain eligible for version control.
